WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.780
PROFESSOR: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.060
So in this lecture,
we want to write down

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.070
the Feynman rules for QCD.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.650
A lot of work happened
to get to this point.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.510
If you recall, we derived
Feynman rules for toy theory,

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:23.310
then we extended this to
QED on the electrodynamics,

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.760
introducing and keeping track
of the spin of particles.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.010
And now in QCD, We have to
do one step in addition.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.060
We have to keep track of
the color of particles.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.670
So color, the charge of QCD,
plays a very important role

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:39.900
here.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.960
If we just investigate this
fundamental process here,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.200
this fundamental
vertex, which is

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.440
very similar to a photon being
radiated from an electron.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.710
Here, we have a gluon
being radiated from quarks.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:57.830
And when this happens, the
quark changes its color.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.460
So here you have a
quark with color blue

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.320
and a quark with color red.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.440
The gluon adds a color.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.200
It adds red, but it also
takes away the blue.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.785
So the quark itself
basically carries two colors.

00:01:11.785 --> 00:01:17.530
It's bi-colored-- one color and
at the same time an anti-color.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.710
A leading order process
here has two vertices.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.570
And this is a
scattering, for example,

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.970
of two quarks which
[INAUDIBLE] through a gluon.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:30.200
All right, so we have
three kinds of charges,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.160
meaning that the quarks
come in red, green, or blue.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.820
So we have to keep track of this
when write down our amplitudes

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.370
or matrix element.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.720
So we do this by just
introducing a new vector.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.340
I call this c here.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:47.780
Very simple-- a three-element
vector for those three colors.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.360
All right, but how
about the gluon itself?

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.100
So QCD is based on a symmetry
group which is called SU3.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.790
So it's basically a rotation
in three dimensional space.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:02.420
There's eight independent
such rotations.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.490
And what you want to
think about is just

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:08.270
moving from one color
state into the next.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:11.360
So you can write them
down as I show here.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.310
And we do this in
linear combination

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:17.220
of color and anti-color.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.940
If you want to keep track of
this, we do this in this form.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.310
We have a vector of A elements.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:26.210
And the gluon is one
of those components.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.610
This is given here for
this very first one.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.420
So let me introduce
the notation here.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.850
Literally, we were
introducing Pauli matrices,

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:40.630
but not for SU2, as a
symmetry group with SU3.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:42.550
They're called the
Gell-Mann matrices,

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.080
and they're just written here.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.870
Again, those are the rotations
I was just talking about.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.230
Those are the rotation from
one color state into the next.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:53.156
There's commutator
relations for this.

00:02:53.156 --> 00:02:55.630
So if you have two of those
Gell-Mann matrices and you

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.090
tried to write down
the commutator,

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.680
you find this 2 times i times
this structure functions,

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:08.470
or structure
constants, times 1--

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.230
well, the next
Gell-Mann records.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.780
So if you're just thinking
about how many combinations are

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.680
there, there's eight matrices.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.880
We have 8 times 8
times 8 combinations

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.770
of those constants here.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:24.970
And so it means that there
is 512 of those constants.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:26.668
Most of them are 0.

00:03:26.668 --> 00:03:28.210
And the ones which
are not 0, they're

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:32.230
listed here, or
combinations of those.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:36.540
All right, so now we're ready to
just write down the QCD Feynman

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:37.470
rules.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.790
Again, we start from
the external lines.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.870
So for the incoming
quark or outgoing quark,

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:48.043
we write our spinor, and
then we keep track of color.

00:03:48.043 --> 00:03:50.210
We do this for quarks and
we do this for anti-quarks

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.080
in the very same way.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.210
For the gluon now, we have
to keep our polarization,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.550
keep track of the
polarization of the gluon

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.080
and also of the color.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.780
And those are the
vectors I just introduced

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.110
on the previous slide.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.465
For the propagators, we
have quarks and anti-quarks

00:04:09.465 --> 00:04:10.950
and gluons.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.990
Gluons are massless,
so the propagator here

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:19.500
looks very much like
the one for the photon.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.140
And the propagator
for the quarks

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:27.210
looks very much like the one
we had for the electron in QED.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.230
The fundamental vertices,
I introduced one already,

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:33.820
but there's two more.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.990
That's because gluon carries
charge and can come to itself.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.270
We already discussed
this in a recitation.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:43.420
So we have those self-couplings
of the gluon here.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.860
And those vertices are as
relevant or more relevant

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.330
even than the one here.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:55.410
All right, so the vertices
come with a vertex factor

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.780
in our Feynman rules.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.680
Again, for this very first
one, we find a very similar one

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.760
as we had before.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.490
You see here this
Gell-Mann matrix.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.030
And let's just call this
a rotation in color.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.910
But we have this three gluon
vertex and the four gluon

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.110
vertex as well.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.880
And those come with
structure functions

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.170
here to keep track of the
permutations, commutations

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:22.660
between the color involved.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:26.410
And it becomes more
complicated for this four gluon

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:32.960
vertex, which has pairs of
structure constants each.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.690
All right, that's
all we need in order

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:40.340
to calculate matrix elements
or amplitudes for QCD.

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:43.280
The rest is just executing
the Feynman rules

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:47.590
as we did before for our toy
theory, and also for QED.

